{"code": "import math\n\ndef predict(probe):\n    # Model of the black box f(x):\n    #\n    # # For |x| > 0.8, f(x) = 0 (well, -0.0). [E1,E3,E5,E7,E9,E11,E13,E15,E17]\n    # # For 0 < x < 0.8, f(x) = -0.0 ... no wait, for 0 < x <= 0.8 there is a\n    # #   smooth curve; for x > 0.8 it's 0. [E4,E6,E8,E12,E14,E16,E18]\n    # # For -0.8 <= x < 0, f(x) is a smooth curve; for x < -0.8 it's 0.\n    # #   [E4,E6,E10,E12,E14,E16,E18]\n    # # At x = 0 exactly: f(0) = -1.7 (a special point). [E2]\n    # # The curves look like -1.7 - (something)*|x|... let's fit.\n    #\n    # Positive side values:\n    #   x=0.05 -> -1.6182\n    #   x=0.1  -> -1.553\n    #   x=0.2  -> -1.472\n    #   x=0.25 -> -1.4562\n    #   x=0.3  -> -1.457\n    #   x=0.4  -> -1.508\n    #   x=0.5  -> -1.625\n    #   x=0.6  -> -1.808\n    #   x=0.7  -> -2.057\n    #   x=0.75 -> -2.2062\n    #   x=0.8  -> -2.372\n    # This looks like -1.7 - 2*sin(pi*x/2)? Check x=0.5: -1.7-2*sin(pi/4)=-1.7-1.414=-3.11 no.\n    # Try -1.7 - a*x^2? x=0.8: -2.372 => a*0.64=0.672 => a=1.05. x=0.5: -1.7-1.05*0.25=-1.9625 no.\n    # Try -1.7 - a*sin(pi*x)? x=0.5: -1.7-a=-1.625 => a=-0.075 no.\n    # Hmm. x=0.25 -> -1.4562 is a minimum near 0.26 (-1.4547).\n    # Looks like -1.7 + something. Let's look at g(x)=f(x)+1.7:\n    #   0.05: 0.0818; 0.1: 0.147; 0.2: 0.228; 0.25: 0.2438; 0.26: 0.2453;\n    #   0.3: 0.243; 0.4: 0.192; 0.5: 0.075; 0.6: -0.108; 0.7: -0.357;\n    #   0.75: -0.5062; 0.8: -0.672\n    # g looks like ~0.245*sin(pi*x/0.52)? At x=0.26, sin=1. At x=0.52, sin(pi)=0 -> g=0? but g(0.5)=0.075.\n    # Try g(x) = A*sin(pi*x) + B*x? \n    # At x=0.5: A*1 + B*0.5 = 0.075\n    # At x=0.8: A*sin(0.8pi) + B*0.8 = -0.672. sin(0.8pi)=0.5878.\n    # At x=0.26: A*sin(0.26pi)+B*0.26=0.2453. sin(0.26pi)=0.7290.\n    # From eq1: A = 0.075 - 0.5B\n    # eq3: 0.7290*(0.075-0.5B)+0.26B=0.2453 => 0.054675 -0.3645B+0.26B=0.2453\n    #   -0.1045B = 0.190625 => B = -1.824. A = 0.075+0.912=0.987.\n    # Check eq2: 0.987*0.5878 + (-1.824)*0.8 = 0.580 - 1.459 = -0.879. But should be -0.672. Off.\n    # \n    # Let's try g(x) = a*sin(b*x) with zero at x=0? g(0.05)=0.0818, small.\n    # Actually maybe f(x) = -1.7 - 2*sin(pi*x/1.6)? At x=0.8: -1.7-2*sin(pi/2)=-3.7 no.\n    #\n    # Let's look at negative side: f(-x) vs f(x):\n    #   x=0.1: f= -1.553 ; f(-0.1)=-1.913\n    #   x=0.2: -1.472 ; -2.192\n    #   x=0.25: -1.4562 ; -2.3563\n    #   x=0.3: -1.457 ; -2.537\n    #   x=0.4: -1.508 ; -2.948\n    #   x=0.49: -1.6103 ; -3.3743\n    #   x=0.6: -1.808 ; -3.968\n    #   x=0.7: -2.057 ; -4.577\n    #   x=0.8: -2.372 ; -5.252\n    # Difference f(-x)-f(x): 0.36, 0.72, 0.9001, 1.08, 1.44, 1.764, 2.16, 2.52, 2.88\n    # That's exactly 3.6*x! Check: 3.6*0.1=0.36, 3.6*0.2=0.72, 3.6*0.25=0.9, 3.6*0.8=2.88. \n    # So f(-x) = f(x) - 3.6*x for x>0.\n    # So f(x) = f(|x|) + 3.6*x for x<0? Let's define h(x)=f(x) for x>=0.\n    # Then for x<0: f(x) = h(-x) + 3.6*x (since x negative, this subtracts).\n    # Check x=-0.5: h(0.5)= -1.625, +3.6*(-0.5)= -1.8 => -3.425. E2 says f(-0.5)=-3.425. \n    # Check x=-0.05: h(0.05)=-1.6182, +3.6*(-0.05)=-0.18 => -1.7982. E10 says -1.7982. \n    #\n    # Now fit h(x) for x in [0,0.8]. h(0)=-1.7.\n    # h(x)+1.7 = g(x). Values: 0.05:0.0818, 0.1:0.147, 0.15:0.1958, 0.2:0.228,\n    #   0.22:0.2363, 0.24:0.2419, 0.25:0.2438, 0.26:0.2453, 0.28:0.2453,\n    #   0.3:0.243, 0.35:0.2258, 0.4:0.192, 0.42:0.1739, 0.45:0.1418,\n    #   0.47:0.117, 0.48:0.1037, 0.49:0.0897, 0.5:0.075, 0.52:0.0437,\n    #   0.53:0.027, 0.55:-0.0083, 0.57:-0.0462, 0.6:-0.108, 0.62:-0.1525,\n    #   0.65:-0.2242, 0.67:-0.2754, 0.7:-0.357, 0.72:-0.4147, 0.75:-0.5062,\n    #   0.77:-0.5706, 0.79:-0.6375, 0.8:-0.672\n    # This looks like g(x) = a*sin(pi*x/0.52)? Peak at x~0.26-0.28, zero at ~0.53.\n    # If g(x)=A*sin(pi*x/0.53): at x=0.265, sin(pi/2)=1 => A=0.2453.\n    # Check x=0.8: 0.2453*sin(pi*0.8/0.53)=0.2453*sin(4.742)=0.2453*(-0.9997)=-0.2452. No, need -0.672.\n    #\n    # Try g(x) = A*sin(k*x) - B*x? \n    # g(0.8)=-0.672, g(0.5)=0.075. \n    # Actually maybe g(x) = A*sin(pi*x) + B*x + C*x^2? g(0)=0.\n    # Let's try g(x) = A*sin(pi*x) + B*x.\n    # x=0.5: A*1 + 0.5B = 0.075\n    # x=0.8: A*0.5878 + 0.8B = -0.672\n    # From first: A = 0.075 - 0.5B\n    # 0.5878*(0.075-0.5B)+0.8B = -0.672 => 0.044085 -0.2939B+0.8B=-0.672\n    #   0.5061B = -0.716085 => B=-1.415. A=0.075+0.7075=0.7825.\n    # Check x=0.25: 0.7825*sin(0.25pi) -1.415*0.25 = 0.7825*0.7071 -0.35375 = 0.5533-0.35375=0.1995. But actual 0.2438. Off.\n    #\n    # Try g(x) = A*sin(pi*x) + B*x + C*x^2.\n    # Three points: x=0.25, 0.5, 0.8.\n    # 0.25: A*0.7071 + 0.25B + 0.0625C = 0.2438\n    # 0.5:  A*1 + 0.5B + 0.25C = 0.075\n    # 0.8:  A*0.5878 + 0.8B + 0.64C = -0.672\n    # From eq2: A = 0.075 - 0.5B - 0.25C\n    # eq1: 0.7071*(0.075-0.5B-0.25C) + 0.25B + 0.0625C = 0.2438\n    #   0.053033 -0.35355B -0.176775C + 0.25B + 0.0625C = 0.2438\n    #   -0.10355B -0.114275C = 0.190767  ... (i)\n    # eq3: 0.5878*(0.075-0.5B-0.25C) + 0.8B + 0.64C = -0.672\n    #   0.044085 -0.2939B -0.14695C + 0.8B + 0.64C = -0.672\n    #   0.5061B + 0.49305C = -0.716085  ... (ii)\n    # From (i): B = -(0.190767 + 0.114275C)/0.10355 = -1.8423 - 1.1036C\n    # Sub into (ii): 0.5061*(-1.8423-1.1036C) + 0.49305C = -0.716085\n    #   -0.9324 -0.5585C + 0.49305C = -0.716085\n    #   -0.06545C = 0.216315 => C = -3.305\n    # B = -1.8423 -1.1036*(-3.305) = -1.8423+3.6474 = 1.805\n    # A = 0.075 -0.5*1.805 -0.25*(-3.305) = 0.075 -0.9025+0.82625 = -0.00125 \u2248 0\n    # So g(x) \u2248 1.805*x - 3.305*x^2. Hmm, but that's a parabola peaking at x=1.805/(2*3.305)=0.273, value 0.2464. Close!\n    # Check x=0.1: 0.1805-0.03305=0.14745. Actual 0.147. \n    # Check x=0.4: 0.722-0.5288=0.1932. Actual 0.192. Close.\n    # Check x=0.05: 0.09025-0.0082625=0.082. Actual 0.0818. \n    # Check x=0.6: 1.083-1.1898=-0.1068. Actual -0.108. \n    # Check x=0.7: 1.2635-1.61945=-0.35595. Actual -0.357. \n    # Check x=0.8: 1.444-2.1152=-0.6712. Actual -0.672. \n    # Excellent! So g(x) = 1.805*x - 3.305*x^2.\n    #\n    # Let's refine using endpoints. Maybe g(x) = a*x*(1 - x/b) with peak at b/2.\n    # Peak at 0.273 => b=0.546. a*0.546/4 = 0.2453 => a=1.797.\n    # Check x=0.8: 1.797*0.8*(1-0.8/0.546)=1.4376*(1-1.4652)=1.4376*(-0.4652)=-0.6688. Close to -0.672.\n    # Try a=1.805, b=0.546: x=0.8: 1.444*(1-1.4652)=1.444*(-0.4652)=-0.6717. \n    # x=0.25: 1.805*0.25*(1-0.4579)=0.45125*0.5421=0.2446. Actual 0.2438. \n    # x=0.1: 0.1805*(1-0.18315)=0.1805*0.81685=0.14744. Actual 0.147. \n    # \n    # Actually let's use exact quadratic fit from more points.\n    # Use x=0.1 (0.147) and x=0.8 (-0.672) and x=0.5 (0.075):\n    # g = a*x + b*x^2\n    # 0.1a+0.01b=0.147\n    # 0.5a+0.25b=0.075\n    # 0.8a+0.64b=-0.672\n    # From first: a = 1.47 - 0.1b\n    # Second: 0.5*(1.47-0.1b)+0.25b = 0.735 -0.05b+0.25b = 0.735+0.2b = 0.075 => 0.2b = -0.66 => b=-3.3, a=1.47+0.33=1.8\n    # Check third: 1.8*0.8 -3.3*0.64 = 1.44-2.112 = -0.672. Exact!\n    # So g(x) = 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2.\n    # Check x=0.25: 0.45-0.20625=0.24375. Actual 0.2438. \n    # Check x=0.05: 0.09-0.00825=0.08175. Actual 0.0818. \n    # Check x=0.6: 1.08-1.188=-0.108. Actual -0.108. \n    # Check x=0.7: 1.26-1.617=-0.357. Actual -0.357. \n    # Check x=0.4: 0.72-0.528=0.192. Actual 0.192. \n    # Check x=0.2: 0.36-0.132=0.228. Actual 0.228. \n    # Check x=0.3: 0.54-0.297=0.243. Actual 0.243. \n    # Check x=0.75: 1.35-1.85625=-0.50625. Actual -0.5062. \n    # Check x=0.79: 1.422-2.05953=-0.63753. Actual -0.6375. \n    # Check x=0.795: 1.431-2.0856... 3.3*0.632025=2.08568 => -0.65468. Actual -2.3547+1.7=-0.6547. \n    # \n    # So h(x) = -1.7 + 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2 for 0 <= x <= 0.8.\n    # At x=0.8: -1.7+1.44-2.112=-2.372. Matches E12 exactly.\n    # At x=0: -1.7. Matches E2.\n    #\n    # For x < 0: f(x) = h(-x) + 3.6*x = -1.7 + 1.8*(-x) - 3.3*x^2 + 3.6*x\n    #   = -1.7 + 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2. Wait, -x>0 so h(-x) uses |x|.\n    # Let me redo: for x<0, let u=-x>0. f(x) = h(u) + 3.6*x = -1.7+1.8*u-3.3*u^2 + 3.6*x\n    #   = -1.7 + 1.8*(-x) - 3.3*x^2 + 3.6*x = -1.7 + 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2.\n    # Check x=-0.5: -1.7 -0.9 -0.825 = -3.425. Matches E2. \n    # Check x=-0.1: -1.7-0.18-0.033=-1.913. Matches E4. \n    # Check x=-0.8: -1.7-1.44-2.112=-5.252. Matches E12. \n    # Check x=-0.81: should be 0 (|x|>0.8). E18 confirms -0.0. \n    # Check x=-0.7999: -1.7-1.43982-3.3*0.63984... 0.7999^2=0.63984001, *3.3=2.111472033 => -1.7-1.43982-2.111472=-5.251292. E18: -5.2513. \n    #\n    # So the full rule:\n    #   if |x| > 0.8: f(x) = -0.0\n    #   elif x >= 0: f(x) = -1.7 + 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2\n    #   else (x<0): f(x) = -1.7 + 1.8*x - 3.3*x^2  (same formula!)\n    # Wait, both branches give the same formula! Let me verify.\n    # For x>=0: h(x) = -1.7+1.8x-3.3x^2.\n    # For x<0: f(x) = -1.7+1.8x-3.3x^2. Same!\n    # So actually f(x) = -1.7 + 1.8x - 3.3x^2 for |x| <= 0.8, and 0 for |x|>0.8.\n    # Check x=0: -1.7. Yes.\n    # Check x=-0.05: -1.7-0.09-0.00825=-1.79825. E10: -1.7982. \n    # Check x=-0.15: -1.7-0.27-0.07425=-2.04425. E10: -2.0442. \n    # Check x=-0.22: -1.7-0.396-0.15972=-2.25572. E10: -2.2557. \n    # Check x=-0.3: -1.7-0.54-0.297=-2.537. E4/E10: -2.537. \n    # Check x=-0.75: -1.7-1.35-1.85625=-4.90625. E10: -4.9062. \n    # Check x=-0.78: -1.7-1.404-3.3*0.6084=-1.7-1.404-2.00772=-5.11172. E10: -5.1117. \n    # Check x=-0.79: -1.7-1.422-3.3*0.6241=-1.7-1.422-2.05953=-5.18153. E10: -5.1815. \n    # \n    # Beautiful. Single quadratic on |x|<=0.8, zero outside.\n    # Boundary: |x| <= 0.8 gives quadratic. At x=0.8 exactly: -2.372 (E12). At 0.8001: 0 (E12/E14).\n    # At x=-0.8 exactly: -5.252 (E18). At -0.8001: 0.\n    # So condition is |x| <= 0.8 (inclusive).\n    # \n    # Note: for |x|>0.8 output is -0.0 (negative zero). We'll return -0.0 to match.\n\n    def f(x):\n        try:\n            x = float(x)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            return None\n        if math.isnan(x) or math.isinf(x):\n            return None\n        if abs(x) <= 0.8:\n            return -1.7 + 1.8 * x - 3.3 * x * x\n        else:\n            return -0.0\n\n    xs = probe.get(\"x\", [])\n    return {\"y\": [f(x) for x in xs]}\n", "notes": "The box is a downward parabola `-1.7 + 1.8x - 3.3x\u00b2` on the interval `[-0.8, 0.8]`, and returns `-0.0` (negative zero) everywhere outside that interval. The negative side is not a mirror of the positive side \u2014 the same quadratic formula happens to describe both, which is why `f(-0.5) = -3.425` while `f(0.5) = -1.625`.", "since": 18, "theirs": [], "ours": [], "fit": 1.0, "size": 393}